1. The 4 Pillars of OOP
	• Encapsulation (Data Hiding): Wrapping data (variables) and code (methods) together into a single unit, keeping fields private and exposing them via public getters/setters.
		○ Real-World Example: An ATM machine. You interact with a simple interface (screen/buttons) to withdraw cash, but the internal cash-counting mechanisms and database connections are hidden from you.
	• Abstraction (Implementation Hiding): Hiding complex implementation details and showing only the essential features to the user.
		○ Real-World Example: Driving a car. You know accelerating makes it go faster, but you don’t need to understand the combustion engine mechanics or fuel injection systems under the hood.
	• Inheritance (Reusability): The mechanism where a new class acquires the properties and behaviors of an existing class.
		○ Real-World Example: A Smartphone. A modern iPhone or Android inherits core features from the original concept of a telephone (calling, texting) but adds its own advanced features (camera, apps).
	• Polymorphism (Many Forms): The ability of a single action or reference to take on multiple forms depending on the context.
		○ Real-World Example: The "Cut" command. Cutting a piece of cloth uses scissors; cutting a cake uses a knife; cutting text in an editor uses a clipboard. The command is the same, but the execution depends on the object.

2. Abstract Class vs. Interface
Feature	Abstract Class	Interface
Methods	Can have abstract, concrete, and static methods.	Historically only abstract. Can now have default, static, and private methods (Java 8+).
Variables	Can have instance variables (private, protected, etc.).	Can only have public static final constants.
Inheritance	A class can extend only one abstract class.	A class can implement multiple interfaces.
Speed	Slightly faster (direct lookup).	Slightly slower (requires search through interface tables).
When to use which?
	• Use an Abstract Class when you want to share a common code base and tightly related state/behavior among closely related objects (e.g., Vehicle $\rightarrow$ Car, Truck).
	• Use an Interface when you want to define a contract or a peripheral capability for completely unrelated classes (e.g., Flyable implemented by both Airplane and Bird).

3. Why Java Doesn't Support Multiple Inheritance with Classes
Java excludes multiple inheritance to avoid the Diamond Problem (ambiguity).
If Class A has a method foo(), and Classes B and C inherit from A and both override foo(), a Class D inheriting from both B and C wouldn't know which version of foo() to invoke.
	Note: Java avoids this with classes entirely. For interfaces (where multiple inheritance of behavior is allowed via default methods), Java forces the developer to manually override and resolve the conflict explicitly if a naming clash occurs.

4. Polymorphism: Static vs. Dynamic
Polymorphism allows one interface to control access to a general class of actions.
Polymorphism is one of the fundamental concepts of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) that allows a single entity to take multiple forms. It enables the same method or interface to behave differently depending on the object involved. Polymorphism improves code flexibility, reusability, and maintainability.
	• Polymorphism means "many forms".
	• It allows the same method to exhibit different behaviors.
	• It is achieved through method overloading and method overriding.

	• Static (Compile-time) Polymorphism: Resolved during compilation. Achieved via Method Overloading (same method name, different parameter signatures).
		○ Example: Math.max(int, int) vs Math.max(double, double).
	• Dynamic (Runtime) Polymorphism: Resolved during runtime. Achieved via Method Overriding (subclass provides a specific implementation of a method declared in its parent class). It relies on Dynamic Method Dispatch.
		○ Example: Animal a = new Dog(); a.makeSound(); (Calls Dog's version of the method at runtime).

5. Abstraction vs. Encapsulation
While they sound similar, they operate at different design levels:
	• Abstraction is a design-level concept. It focuses on what an object does rather than how it does it (achieved via Interfaces and Abstract classes).
	• Encapsulation is an implementation-level concept. It focuses on how to secure the data by hiding the internal state and restricting direct access (achieved via private access modifiers and getters/setters).

6. Aggregation vs. Composition
Both are forms of association ("has-a" relationships), but they differ in lifecycle dependency:
	• Aggregation (Weak Bonding): The child object can exist independently of the parent object.
		○ Example: A Department has Professors. If the department closes, the professors still exist.
	• Composition (Strong Bonding): The child object cannot exist without the parent object.
		○ Example: A House has Rooms. If the house is demolished, the rooms cease to exist.

7. Why is String Immutable in Java?
	1. String Pool (Memory Efficiency): Java shares string literals in a common pool. If strings were mutable, changing the value for one reference would silently corrupt the data for all other references pointing to the same literal.
	2. Security: Strings are heavily used for network connections, file paths, and database URLs. If strings were mutable, an attacker could change a validated path/URL right before execution.
	3. Thread Safety: Since they cannot be modified, strings are inherently thread-safe and don't require synchronization.
	4. Caching HashCode: The hashcode of a string is cached when it's created. This makes it incredibly fast when used as keys in data structures like HashMap.

8. final vs. finally vs. finalize
	• final (Keyword): Used to apply restrictions. A final class cannot be inherited, a final method cannot be overridden, and a final variable cannot be reassigned.
	• finally (Block): A block used in exception handling (try-catch-finally) that always executes (unless JVM crashes via System.exit()), ensuring clean-up tasks like closing database connections take place.
	• finalize() (Method): A method belonging to the Object class that the Garbage Collector calls right before destroying an object to let it perform cleanup. (Deprecated since Java 9).

9. Marker Interface & Real-time Use Cases
A Marker Interface is an empty interface with no methods or fields. It serves as a tag to deliver a metadata instruction to the JVM or a compiler.
Real-time Use Cases:
	• Serializable: Tells the JVM that this object's state can be converted into a byte stream.
	• Cloneable: Signals to the Object.clone() method that it is legal to make a field-for-field copy of instances of that class.
	• RandomAccess: Used by collections to indicate they support fast (constant time) random access (like ArrayList, but unlike LinkedList).

10. Can We Override a Static Method?
No. You cannot override a static method because method overriding is based on runtime dynamic binding, whereas static methods are bound at compile-time using the class type (static binding).
If you declare a static method with the exact same signature in a child class, it is called Method Hiding, not overriding. The method that gets called depends entirely on the reference type, not the actual object type.

11. Effective Final Concept (Java 8+)
A variable is considered effectively final if it is not explicitly declared with the final keyword, but its value never changes after initialization.
Java introduced this to allow local variables to be used inside lambda expressions or anonymous inner classes without forcing developers to explicitly write final everywhere—provided the variable remains unmutated.

12. Checked Exceptions: Pros & Cons
A Checked Exception is an exception that is checked by the compiler at compile-time (e.g., IOException, SQLException). The developer is forced to handle it using a try-catch block or declare it in the method signature using throws.
	• Advantages: Enhances application reliability by forcing the developer to anticipate and gracefully recover from predictable, external failures (like a missing file).
	• Disadvantages: Can clutter the codebase with excessive boilerplate code (try-catch blocks) and can expose implementation details upward through a call stack via explicit throws declarations.

13. String s = new String("hello"); — Objects Created
This statement creates 2 objects:
	1. One in the Heap Memory: Created due to the explicit use of the new keyword.
	2. One in the String Constant Pool (SCP): Created for the literal "hello", provided that "hello" does not already exist in the pool. (If it already exists in the pool, only 1 object is created in the heap).

14. Dynamic Class Loading
Dynamic class loading is the process of loading a Java class into memory at runtime on demand, rather than during compilation. It is performed using Class.forName() or via a custom ClassLoader.
Where is it used?
	• Database Drivers: Loading a specific JDBC driver at runtime based on user configuration (Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver")).
	• Plugin Architectures: Enabling IDEs (like Eclipse or IntelliJ) or web servers (like Tomcat) to load third-party extensions/servlets dynamically without restarting.

15. Methods Available in the Object Class
The java.lang.Object class contains 11 methods:
	1. public final Class<?> getClass() — Returns the runtime class of the object.
	2. public int hashCode() — Returns a hash code value for the object.
	3. public boolean equals(Object obj) — Compares two objects for equality.
	4. protected Object clone() — Creates and returns a copy of this object.
	5. public String toString() — Returns a string representation of the object.
	6. public final void notify() — Wakes up a single thread waiting on this object’s monitor.
	7. public final void notifyAll() — Wakes up all threads waiting on this object’s monitor.
	8. public final void wait() — Causes the current thread to wait until notified.
	9. public final void wait(long timeout) — Waits for a specific duration.
	10. public final void wait(long timeout, int nanos) — Waits for a precise duration down to nanoseconds.
	11. protected void finalize() — Called by the garbage collector (Deprecated).

