16. What is the difference between Collection and Collections?
	• Collection: An interface (java.util.Collection) that serves as the root interface for data structures like List, Set, and Queue.
	• Collections: A utility class (java.util.Collections) consisting entirely of static methods that operate on or return collections (e.g., Collections.sort(), Collections.reverse(), Collections.unmodifiableList()).
17. Why is Map not part of the Collection interface?
The Collection interface handles a group of individual, single elements. A Map operates on key-value pairs (). Because Map requires different method signatures (like put(K key, V value) instead of add(E element)), it is incompatible with the core Collection contract and is kept structurally separate.
18. Difference between List, Set, and Map?
Feature	List	Set	Map
Element Type	Single elements	Single elements	Key-Value pairs
Duplicates	Allows duplicates	Unique elements only	Unique keys; duplicate values allowed
Order	Maintains insertion order	Order depends on implementation	Order depends on implementation
Null Elements	Multiple null allowed	At most one null (usually)	One null key; multiple null values
