19. Difference between ArrayList and LinkedList (with time complexity)?
	• ArrayList: Backed by a dynamically resizing array.
		○ Search/Retrieval (get): constant time because it supports index-based lookup.
		○ Insertion/Deletion: in the worst case due to element shifting.
	• LinkedList: Backed by a doubly-linked list.
		○ Search/Retrieval (get): because it must traverse the list node by node.
		○ Insertion/Deletion: once the position is reached (just updating node pointers).
20. How does ArrayList differ from Vector? When to use CopyOnWriteArrayList?
	• ArrayList vs Vector: ArrayList is not synchronized (fast, not thread-safe). Vector is synchronized (slow, legacy, thread-safe). ArrayList grows by 50% when full, whereas Vector doubles (100%) its array size.
	• When to use CopyOnWriteArrayList: Use it in multi-threaded environments where reads vastly outnumber writes. Every write operation clones the underlying array, making writes expensive, but reads are completely lock-free and blazing fast.
21. Difference between HashSet, LinkedHashSet, and TreeSet?
	• HashSet: Offers the best performance; elements are stored in an unpredictable order.
	• LinkedHashSet: Maintains the insertion order of elements using a doubly-linked list running through the hash buckets.
	• TreeSet: Sorts elements in natural sorting order (or via a custom Comparator). It is backed by a Red-Black tree structure.
22. How does HashSet internally prevent duplicates?
Internally, a HashSet uses a HashMap to store its elements. When you call add(element), the HashSet puts the element as a Key in the map (map.put(element, PRESENT)), where PRESENT is a dummy object value. Since HashMap keys must be unique, duplicate additions are naturally rejected.

23. Difference between HashMap, LinkedHashMap, and TreeMap?
	• HashMap: No order guarantees for keys; fastest lookup ( average).
	• LinkedHashMap: Maintains insertion order (or access order) of keys.
	• TreeMap: Sorts keys in natural ascending order or by a Comparator ( performance).
24. How does HashMap work internally?
	1. Hashing: When put(key, value) is called, the key’s hashCode() is calculated and passed to a hash function to determine the specific array index (bucket).
	2. Buckets: The map structure is an array of nodes (buckets).
	3. Collision: If two different keys map to the same bucket index, they are stored as a LinkedList at that bucket. In Java 8+, if a bucket's size exceeds 8 and the overall map capacity , the LinkedList transforms into a Balanced Tree (Red-Black Tree) to optimize worst-case performance from to .
	4. Resizing: When total elements exceed the capacity threshold, the array size is doubled, and all existing elements are rehashed into the new array.
25. Why is HashMap not thread-safe?
HashMap methods are not synchronized. If multiple threads access and modify it concurrently (e.g., during a resize operation), it can lead to data corruption, race con
ditions, or structural corruption (like creating cyclic loops in a bucket's linked list, leading to infinite loops during a get() call).
26. What is load factor in HashMap? What happens when it's exceeded?
	• Load Factor: A measure (default is 0.75) that dictates when the map should grow. It represents the ratio of structural fullness ().
	• Exceeded: When the number of elements exceeds this threshold, a resize (rehashing) occurs. The underlying array size doubles, and all keys are remanned to their new bucket indexes.
27. What is the difference between HashMap and ConcurrentHashMap?
	• HashMap is not thread-safe. Locking it requires synchronizing the entire object.
	• ConcurrentHashMap is fully thread-safe and highly concurrent. It avoids global locks, allowing multiple threads to read and write simultaneously without locking the entire map.
28. How does ConcurrentHashMap work internally?
In modern Java (8+), ConcurrentHashMap uses a fine-grained locking strategy via synchronized blocks on individual bucket heads along with Compare-And-Swap (CAS) operations. If a bucket is empty, a node is inserted using lock-free CAS. If a bucket is populated, it only locks that specific bucket node, allowing other threads to safely modify different buckets simultaneously.
29. Can we use a mutable object as a key in HashMap? Why or why not?
No, it is highly discouraged. If a key's fields mutate after insertion, its hashCode() changes. When you try to look it up later, the map will look in the wrong bucket based on the new hash code, making your original value unretrievable and causing a silent memory leak.
30. How does TreeMap maintain sorting?
TreeMap uses a Red-Black tree (a self-balancing binary search tree). Every time a key-value pair is inserted, it traverses the tree comparing keys using either the key's Comparable implementation (compareTo()) or a provided custom Comparator object to place it in the correct sorted node position.

31. Difference between fail-fast and fail-safe iterators?
	• Fail-Fast: Iterates over the collection's direct data structure. Throws a ConcurrentModificationException immediately if the collection is structurally modified (added/removed elements) during iteration outside the iterator's own methods (e.g., ArrayList iterator).
	• Fail-Safe (Safe-Failure): Operates on a cloned copy or a view of the collection data (e.g., CopyOnWriteArrayList iterator). It does not throw exceptions if the original collection changes during iteration.
32. Why are equals() and hashCode() important in Collections?
They define the contract for object identity and bucket allocation in hashed collections (HashMap, HashSet).
	• hashCode() tells the structure which bucket to look in.
	• equals() tells the structure exactly which object matches within that bucket. If you fail to override both correctly, hashed collections will break and fail to locate your objects.
33. If two objects have the same hashCode(), what will equals() return?
It can return either true or false. A hash collision happens when two entirely distinct objects happen to generate the exact same integer hash code. In that scenario, equals() evaluates whether they are actually structurally identical.
34. If equals() returns true, will hashCode() always be the same?
Yes. According to the official Java language specification contract, if two objects are deemed equal by the equals(Object) method, calling hashCode() on each of them must produce the exact same integer result.



35. What are the major features introduced in Java 8?
	• Lambda Expressions: Enables treating functionality as a method argument (functional programming).
	• Stream API: Provides a functional way to process collections of objects (filtering, mapping, reducing).
	• Functional Interfaces: Interfaces with exactly one abstract method (annotated with @FunctionalInterface).
	• Default and Static Methods: Allows writing method implementations inside interfaces.
	• Optional Class: A container object used to avoid explicit NullPointerException checks.
	• New Date and Time API: Implements a thread-safe, immutable calendar system under java.time (e.g., LocalDate, LocalTime).
36. What are functional interfaces? Name some built-in ones.
A functional interface is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method. It can have any number of default or static methods.
Common built-in functional interfaces in java.util.function:
	• Predicate<T>: Takes one argument and returns a boolean (abstract method: test(T t)).
	• Function<T, R>: Takes one argument and returns a result (abstract method: apply(T t)).
	• Consumer<T>: Takes one argument and returns nothing/void (abstract method: accept(T t)).
	• Supplier<T>: Takes no arguments and returns a value (abstract method: get()).
37. What are default methods in interfaces? What was used before Java 8 instead?
	• Default Methods: Methods inside an interface that have a body, prefixed with the default keyword. They allow developers to add new utility methods to existing interfaces without breaking the classes that already implement them.
	• Before Java 8: Abstract classes or Companion Utility Classes were used. For example, the Collection interface had a companion Collections utility class filled with static methods to provide implementations.
38. What happens if two interfaces have the same default method and a class implements both?
This causes a compiler error due to the "Diamond Problem" (the compiler doesn't know which method version to inherit).
To resolve this, the implementing class must override the conflicting method and manually specify which interface's method to use, or write its own implementation completely:
Java

@Override
public void log() {
    InterfaceA.super.log(); // Explicitly chooses InterfaceA's implementation
}
39. What is the difference between map() and flatMap() in Streams?
	• map(): Used for transformation. It takes an element		 and maps it to a single value ($1 \rightarrow 1$). It returns a stream of values (e.g., transforming a Stream<String> to a Stream<Integer> by string length).
	• flatMap(): Used for transformation + flattening. It maps each element to a stream of values and flattens multiple streams into a single stream ($1 \rightarrow \text{Many}$). It is used to handle nested structures, like converting a Stream<List<String>> into a flat Stream<String>.
40. Difference between terminal and intermediate operations in Streams?
	• Intermediate Operations: Transform a stream into another stream (e.g., filter(), map(), sorted()). They are lazy; they do not execute until a terminal operation is called.
	• Terminal Operations: Produce a non-stream result or a side effect (e.g., collect(), forEach(), count(), reduce()). Calling a terminal operation eagerly triggers the pipeline processing and consumes the stream, after which the stream cannot be reused.

41. What is Optional? What problem does it solve?
	• Optional<T>: A single-value container object that may or may not contain a non-null value.
	• Problem Solved: It explicitly handles the absence of a value, safely replacing boilerplate if (obj != null) checks and minimizing the occurrences of runtime NullPointerException crashes.
42. Difference between isPresent() and ifPresent() in Optional?
	• isPresent(): A boolean check method. It returns true if a value is contained, requiring an additional .get() call to fetch it (imperative style).
	• ifPresent(Consumer action): A functional execution method. It accepts a lambda consumer block that runs only if the value is actually present, eliminating the need for an explicit conditional branch.
43. What are the new features in Java 11 and Java 17?
Java 11 Major Features:
	• var in Lambda Parameters: Allows using local variable syntax inside lambda parameters.
	• New String Methods: isBlank(), lines(), strip(), repeat(n).
	• Native HTTP Client: Standardized, non-blocking HTTP client supporting HTTP/2.
	• Run Source Files Directly: Single-file source code programs can be launched directly via java HelloWorld.java without explicit manual compilation.
Java 17 Major Features (LTS):
	• Sealed Classes: Dictates exactly which subclasses are permitted to extend or implement a given class or interface (sealed ... permits).
	• Records: Transparent data-carrier carrier classes that auto-generate boilerplate constructors, getters, equals(), hashCode(), and toString().
	• Pattern Matching for switch: Enables checking types directly inside a switch block statement.
	• Text Blocks: Multi-line string literals enclosed in triple quotes (""") that preserve formatting naturally.

44. What is the difference between Stream and ParallelStream? Have you used ParallelStream in a real project?
	• Difference: A standard Stream executes sequentially on a single thread. A ParallelStream leverages the JVM's shared ForkJoinPool.commonPool() to split the data stream into chunks and process them concurrently across multiple CPU threads.
	• Real Project Usage: Yes, I utilize them in production tasks specifically for CPU-bound operations involving isolated datasets, such as parsing a massive, read-only collection of data strings into structural database records concurrently.
Note: They should be avoided for I/O operations, network requests, or database lookups, as the shared ForkJoinPool can easily block and degrade the entire application's performance.

